data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR is a genetic editing tool, first found in bacteria, where it protects against virus invasions [1].\n- A guide RNA identifies matching DNA, and a crRNA:tracrRNA:Cas9 complex cuts the target DNA [1][3].\n- Cas9 cuts the targeted DNA, shutting the gene off; modified Cas9 can instead activate gene expression [3].\n- Scientists commonly use CRISPR to \"knock out\" genes, and it can add repair templates to replace disease-causing mutations [2].\n- CRISPR SWAPnDROP extends genome editing to large-scale DNA transfer between bacterial species, shown in *E. coli* [4]."}

